      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "We present a case report of thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura/hemolytic uremic syndrome (TTP/HUS) developing in a kidney/pancreas transplant recipient after the initiation of treatment with clopidogrel for symptomatic coronary artery disease."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "A 35-year-old male kidney/pancreas recipient developed unstable angina 5 years after transplantation. The patient was treated with clopidogrel as adjunct therapy. A TTP/HUS condition developed, was diagnosed early, and successfully reversed with the implementation of plasmapheresis and cessation of clopidogrel and cyclosporine A."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "The patient continues taking cyclosporine A with good renal function 6 months after the incident, and successfully underwent coronary artery by-pass grafting 3 months after the event."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "DISCUSSION",
          "content": "This case demonstrates that early identification and treatment can reverse the TTP/HUS process associated with thienopyridine-derived agents. We strongly recommend that drugs of the thienopyridine class be used cautiously in transplant recipients, especially those taking calcineurin-inhibitors."
        }
      ]},
